1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Harvesting provides humans with over 2 billion tons of fruit and vegetables every year.

2
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
It's one of the most crucial industries on the planet.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
It's dangerous work up there. One slip and they could end up in a world of hurt.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:25,000
These high-tech wonders have transformed America's 2 million farms into a $140 billion industry.

5
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
You're talking about the ability to harvest 4,000 or so bushels an hour?

6
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,040
From optical fruit sizers to computer-controlled corn picking

7
00:00:33,041 --> 00:00:38,040
combines, you'll never shop for produce the same way again

8
00:00:38,041 --> 00:00:39,160
on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:57,340
Every year, from spring until fall,

10
00:00:57,341 --> 00:00:59,840
California's fruit harvest is in high gear.

11
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:08,700
At Reimer Farms in California's San Joaquin Valley,

12
00:01:08,701 --> 00:01:10,080
they're picking nectarines.

13
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:16,300
For all growers, stretching the harvest season

14
00:01:16,301 --> 00:01:18,900
means raising different varieties of the same fruit.

15
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:22,200
We have varieties planted specifically

16
00:01:22,201 --> 00:01:23,940
throughout the different times of the season

17
00:01:23,941 --> 00:01:25,760
to fulfill those different time slots

18
00:01:25,761 --> 00:01:28,200
for providing fruit to the marketplace.

19
00:01:28,201 --> 00:01:30,120
So a typical variety of nectarines

20
00:01:30,121 --> 00:01:32,120
may harvest for two to three weeks.

21
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:36,420
A good harvest depends on fruit

22
00:01:36,421 --> 00:01:38,140
that looks and tastes its best.

23
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:42,640
To add maximum appeal to a nectarine,

24
00:01:42,641 --> 00:01:45,580
most growers line the orchard rows with strips of mylar.

25
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:49,600
The reflective surface concentrates the sun's rays

26
00:01:49,601 --> 00:01:53,040
into the tree canopy, promoting photosynthesis

27
00:01:53,041 --> 00:01:54,600
and increasing the sugar content

28
00:01:54,601 --> 00:01:56,500
and color quality of the fruit.

29
00:01:56,501 --> 00:01:57,960
And that's when the fruit's actually going

30
00:01:57,961 --> 00:01:59,460
through its sizing process.

31
00:01:59,461 --> 00:02:02,460
It's when it's developing its sugar, developing its color.

32
00:02:02,461 --> 00:02:04,740
And so we're trying to maximize its environment

33
00:02:04,741 --> 00:02:08,200
during that particular critical time point before harvest.

34
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:11,460
As the workers begin picking,

35
00:02:11,461 --> 00:02:12,960
growers will spot check the fruit

36
00:02:12,961 --> 00:02:15,240
to verify their decision to start harvesting.

37
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,460
So what we'll do when we're trying to decide

38
00:02:19,461 --> 00:02:20,860
whether we're harvesting properly

39
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:22,700
is we'll sample them with a penetrometer

40
00:02:22,701 --> 00:02:24,160
to see what the flesh firmness is

41
00:02:24,161 --> 00:02:27,580
to determine whether we are picking the level we want.

42
00:02:27,581 --> 00:02:31,080
So we just quickly just shave a piece of the skin off,

43
00:02:31,081 --> 00:02:34,120
and then we use this plunger to push in there.

44
00:02:34,121 --> 00:02:36,360
And the force that it takes to push that in

45
00:02:36,361 --> 00:02:38,520
will then determine the flesh firmness.

46
00:02:38,521 --> 00:02:40,280
This one rated at 11.

47
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:45,560
Picking is no longer the last step in the harvest.

48
00:02:45,561 --> 00:02:48,080
In many cases, it's just the beginning.

49
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:53,400
This 10-acre packing facility

50
00:02:53,401 --> 00:02:59,880
operates three state-of-the-art packing lines for boxing peaches, plums, blueberries, apples,

51
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:07,720
and many other fruits. Today it's nectarines and pomegranates.

52
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:16,280
In one shift, the workers can pack up to 120,000 boxes of fruit on two lines,

53
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:22,040
all of it sorted according to size, color, and degree of ripeness.

54
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,400
We become almost the warehouse for the grocery stores,

55
00:03:26,401 --> 00:03:29,400
so they'll give us an order and give us only like a one or two

56
00:03:29,401 --> 00:03:32,400
day lead time to pack it, put it on a truck, and get it over

57
00:03:32,401 --> 00:03:35,400
to anywhere in the United States.

58
00:03:35,401 --> 00:03:38,400
Once a piece of fruit is picked, its limited lifespan

59
00:03:38,401 --> 00:03:42,400
sparks the race to preserve freshness.

60
00:03:42,401 --> 00:03:46,400
Here, sophisticated post-harvest handling is a way to stall for

61
00:03:46,401 --> 00:03:50,400
time, starting with a cold shower.

62
00:03:50,401 --> 00:03:54,940
The hydro cooler pours frigid 32-degree water over the fruit

63
00:03:54,941 --> 00:03:58,020
to reduce its internal temperature and slow down decay.

64
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:02,380
This is absolutely crucial in maintaining good quality.

65
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:05,220
In the harvest season in California, in the summertime,

66
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:08,000
our temperatures can be well over 90 degrees Fahrenheit,

67
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:09,580
even 100 degrees Fahrenheit.

68
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:12,400
And the fruit will lose quality quite quickly,

69
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:14,660
will dry and lose sugars.

70
00:04:14,661 --> 00:04:22,420
The cooled fruit quickly makes its way onto the sorting line for a quick wash and wax.

71
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:28,660
After it's rinsed, it's sprayed with a vegetable oil product

72
00:04:28,661 --> 00:04:32,180
to help prevent moisture loss during the long journey to the supermarket.

73
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:42,500
Before sizing begins, inspectors use their highly trained senses to remove the subpar fruit.

74
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:47,940
As they touch it, they're looking for a soft shoulder or soft tip on the fruit

75
00:04:47,941 --> 00:04:52,820
that would bruise or damage easily and could later decay in transit.

76
00:04:52,821 --> 00:04:56,900
So that piece of fruit that's a little bit too soft is then removed.

77
00:04:56,901 --> 00:05:05,140
The rejects are diverted and will later produce juices, jams, and other processed products.

78
00:05:05,141 --> 00:05:13,660
The fruit that passes inspection continues its journey to the high-speed sorting line.

79
00:05:13,661 --> 00:05:21,580
For centuries, farmers have sorted fruit to achieve uniform shipping weights and to satisfy

80
00:05:21,581 --> 00:05:23,207
consumer preference.

81
00:05:23,208 --> 00:05:28,900
The first packing houses used crude sizers with wooden slats or holes,

82
00:05:28,901 --> 00:05:31,520
for which the smaller sized fruits were dropped.

83
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:42,260
Today, the optical fruit sizer is a faster, more accurate method.

84
00:05:42,261 --> 00:05:46,960
The fruit is first diverted onto a single file cup conveyor, moving at about 10 pieces

85
00:05:46,961 --> 00:05:49,800
per second.

86
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:55,680
As it passes into the sorter, each piece is rotated and photographed by two lenses.

87
00:05:55,681 --> 00:06:00,960
An infrared lens gathers shape and dimensional data, and a color lens collects the color

88
00:06:00,961 --> 00:06:03,700
qualities of the fruit.

89
00:06:03,701 --> 00:06:08,880
The camera quickly snaps 25 photos to create a two-dimensional projection of the entire fruit.

90
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:15,440
From this picture, the computer interprets its exact size, shape, and color.

91
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:20,060
Now we've got a full look at it, and then we can determine if the amount of color that we see,

92
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:23,880
red versus yellow, is the appropriate color that's desirable for packing.

93
00:06:23,881 --> 00:06:30,000
And that's helpful in markets where we want to send fruit of a uniform size, color, and shape.

94
00:06:30,001 --> 00:06:36,500
Once the computer obtains the mugshots of a certain piece of fruit, a timing device

95
00:06:36,501 --> 00:06:41,700
triggers a lifting finger that deposits the fruit into a specific lane according to size

96
00:06:41,701 --> 00:06:45,040
and color.

97
00:06:45,041 --> 00:06:51,600
This high-tech sorter delivers nearly identical fruit to each of many packing lines.

98
00:06:51,601 --> 00:06:55,720
After a final human inspection, the fruit is hand-packed in a variety of boxes.

99
00:06:55,721 --> 00:07:04,620
Then it's conveyed by forklift into a cold storage room where it's stored at an ideal

100
00:07:04,621 --> 00:07:09,100
or optimum temperature that actually puts the fruit to sleep.

101
00:07:09,101 --> 00:07:13,120
Once the fruit is in that condition or in that state, it's in the best environment to

102
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:19,160
be transported from California to the various markets in the Midwest or the East Coast.

103
00:07:19,161 --> 00:07:25,360
The packing house can even hold boxed fruit at a higher temperature to force ripening.

104
00:07:25,361 --> 00:07:34,480
Sophisticated post-harvest handling of fruit is a progression and a ritual that has remained

105
00:07:34,481 --> 00:07:37,980
otherwise unchanged over thousands of years.

106
00:07:37,981 --> 00:07:41,480
This Egyptian tomb drawing dated at 1900 BCE

107
00:07:41,481 --> 00:07:44,504
depicts two workers harvesting a fig tree.

108
00:07:44,505 --> 00:07:47,600
The key components of harvest are the same.

109
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:51,724
One's picking the fruit and then putting it into a basket.

110
00:07:51,725 --> 00:07:54,320
The other is sorting the fruit and putting

111
00:07:54,321 --> 00:07:56,518
it into a consumer package.

112
00:07:56,519 --> 00:08:01,320
The practices involved in picking and packing are basically

113
00:08:02,060 --> 00:08:09,560
still the same and haven't changed.

114
00:08:09,561 --> 00:08:14,053
Ancient Egypt is one of the birthplaces of horticulture.

115
00:08:14,054 --> 00:08:16,300
The first growers learned to

116
00:08:16,301 --> 00:08:20,880
irrigate their fields by tapping the Nile River's seasonal floods.

117
00:08:20,881 --> 00:08:23,100
But without modern technologies,

118
00:08:23,101 --> 00:08:26,100
these growers were faced with the problem of spoilage.

119
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:30,600
They didn't have refrigeration, they didn't have canning,

120
00:08:30,601 --> 00:08:34,600
they didn't have these other processes to preserve the harvested crop.

121
00:08:34,601 --> 00:08:39,600
So the crop had to either be consumed fresh soon after harvest

122
00:08:39,601 --> 00:08:43,600
or dried and stored dry.

123
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,100
China was the birthplace of temperate fruits,

124
00:08:47,101 --> 00:08:51,100
such as peaches and pears, more than 4,000 years ago.

125
00:08:51,101 --> 00:08:53,980
The Greeks and Romans oversaw a thriving fruit trade

126
00:08:53,981 --> 00:08:57,180
throughout the Mediterranean.

127
00:08:57,181 --> 00:09:00,480
When Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 CE,

128
00:09:00,481 --> 00:09:02,980
the Romans were cultivating extensive orchards,

129
00:09:02,981 --> 00:09:06,000
including peaches, citrus trees, and grapes

130
00:09:06,001 --> 00:09:09,380
on the volcano's slopes.

131
00:09:09,381 --> 00:09:11,100
During the Middle Ages, monks were

132
00:09:11,101 --> 00:09:13,580
the most sophisticated growers.

133
00:09:13,581 --> 00:09:16,900
They practiced the skills of tree grafting and crossbreeding

134
00:09:16,901 --> 00:09:18,860
to produce new varieties of fruit trees.

135
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:24,180
The colonists brought Europe's fruit tree saplings to the New World,

136
00:09:24,181 --> 00:09:29,020
but the true American orchard revolution was again fueled by monks.

137
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:36,280
As the Spanish moved north into California and established missions in the 1600s,

138
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:44,080
the monks took advantage of the dry climate and raised large orchards of grapes, figs, peaches, pears, and apricots.

139
00:09:44,081 --> 00:09:48,080
As settlers moved to California in the 1800s,

140
00:09:48,081 --> 00:09:52,080
the orchards fanned out to supply the new urban centers

141
00:09:52,081 --> 00:09:55,080
of Los Angeles and San Francisco.

142
00:09:55,081 --> 00:09:58,080
Growers also planted in the Sierra foothills

143
00:09:58,081 --> 00:10:01,080
to feed the influx of gold miners.

144
00:10:01,081 --> 00:10:05,080
California fruit production exploded in the 1890s

145
00:10:05,081 --> 00:10:07,080
when refrigerated railroad cars allowed growers

146
00:10:07,081 --> 00:10:11,080
to ship fresh fruit to the huge East Coast markets.

147
00:10:11,081 --> 00:10:18,980
But in the coming decades, the small family-operated farm would nearly vanish.

148
00:10:18,981 --> 00:10:22,480
Farms began to get bigger primarily because of competition.

149
00:10:22,481 --> 00:10:30,620
There's a continuing pressure to reduce the price of fruits and vegetables at retail market.

150
00:10:30,621 --> 00:10:35,800
And that means the farms generally have to get larger in size to take advantage of economies

151
00:10:35,801 --> 00:10:41,000
of scale.

152
00:10:41,001 --> 00:10:45,445
Large vertically integrated growing and packing operations are now the standard in U.S.

153
00:10:45,446 --> 00:10:46,146
fruit

154
00:10:46,147 --> 00:10:47,180
production.

155
00:10:47,181 --> 00:10:52,280
But labor remains the most unpredictable and expensive cost for growers, especially when

156
00:10:52,281 --> 00:11:02,400
balancing the need to pay a decent wage while providing affordable produce for consumers.

157
00:11:02,401 --> 00:11:07,960
Beginning in the 1960s, engineers from the University of California at Davis began testing

158
00:11:07,961 --> 00:11:13,360
a multitude of mechanical fruit harvesters.

159
00:11:13,361 --> 00:11:15,860
Various tree shakers and shake and catch machines

160
00:11:15,861 --> 00:11:17,920
were very effective at harvesting fruit quickly.

161
00:11:20,920 --> 00:11:23,420
But the percentage of damaged fruit was unacceptable.

162
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:29,400
Half a century later, there are still

163
00:11:29,401 --> 00:11:31,820
no commercially viable mechanical harvesters

164
00:11:31,821 --> 00:11:34,300
for picking many types of fresh fruit.

165
00:11:34,301 --> 00:11:37,240
One solution may be a man-machine interface

166
00:11:37,241 --> 00:11:40,480
that allows laborers to more efficiently access the tree canopy.

167
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:47,740
Here, a pear grower and engineers from UC Davis

168
00:11:47,741 --> 00:11:50,160
are testing a modified European-built platform

169
00:11:50,161 --> 00:11:51,220
in Northern California.

170
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:58,040
The machine offers six movable picking platforms

171
00:11:58,041 --> 00:12:03,480
at three different heights, allowing the harvesters

172
00:12:03,481 --> 00:12:08,040
to move laterally or up and down inside the tree canopy.

173
00:12:08,041 --> 00:12:10,980
So the workers, instead of placing fruit into bags

174
00:12:10,981 --> 00:12:13,520
and delivering it to bins, would place fruit two or three

175
00:12:13,521 --> 00:12:15,700
at a time into little feeder conveyors

176
00:12:15,701 --> 00:12:17,480
that fed into a main elevator.

177
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:23,240
At the top of the main elevator, the fruit

178
00:12:23,241 --> 00:12:25,520
would go into what's called a bin filler,

179
00:12:25,521 --> 00:12:29,040
where the fruit is lowered and placed into a rotating bin,

180
00:12:29,041 --> 00:12:31,920
which ensures even distribution of the fruit in the bin.

181
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:41,280
By eliminating the need to carry heavy ladders and picking bags, the platform might expand

182
00:12:41,281 --> 00:12:43,040
the potential workforce.

183
00:12:43,041 --> 00:12:49,400
It could also improve employee safety by reducing hazardous working conditions and injuries

184
00:12:49,401 --> 00:12:56,420
from ladder falls, a major problem for many growers.

185
00:12:56,421 --> 00:13:00,680
The harvesting platform has already gained a firm footing in Europe, where hand labor

186
00:13:00,681 --> 00:13:02,680
has been in short supply for decades.

187
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:13,600
In the Midwest, America's biggest harvest has eliminated handpicking and put the farmer at the

188
00:13:13,601 --> 00:13:19,968
wheel of a computer-controlled maximum efficiency machine.

189
00:13:19,969 --> 00:13:24,360
Nebraska John Sandahl is harvesting corn

190
00:13:24,361 --> 00:13:26,560
in a John Deere 12-row combine.

191
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:32,300
The air-conditioned cab may seem cushy,

192
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:36,200
and combining corn may look a bit like mowing the lawn,

193
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:38,640
but this harvest demands a sharp eye

194
00:13:38,641 --> 00:13:40,300
and fast decision-making.

195
00:13:41,420 --> 00:13:44,960
While harvesting corn, we're scanning the corn head

196
00:13:44,961 --> 00:13:47,140
all the time, watching for objects in the field,

197
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:50,420
make sure that all the row units are functioning properly,

198
00:13:50,540 --> 00:13:52,080
pulling the corn stalks down through.

199
00:13:52,081 --> 00:13:57,320
And this corner post monitors all the functions of the machine.

200
00:13:57,321 --> 00:14:02,380
The first one down here has the engine RPM, the speed that I'm traveling.

201
00:14:02,381 --> 00:14:05,060
The middle one is the grain loss monitor.

202
00:14:05,061 --> 00:14:06,060
That's very important.

203
00:14:06,061 --> 00:14:14,280
It keeps track, monitors how much grain the combine is losing out the back.

204
00:14:14,281 --> 00:14:19,580
A small percentage of the harvested corn drops back in the field, and the operator minimizes

205
00:14:19,581 --> 00:14:21,920
these losses by slowing down when necessary.

206
00:14:23,840 --> 00:14:26,900
The modern combine is a factory on wheels.

207
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:29,800
As corn stalks enter the corn head,

208
00:14:30,260 --> 00:14:32,420
a series of rollers pull down the stalks

209
00:14:32,421 --> 00:14:33,540
and snap off the ears.

210
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:36,320
An auger moves the ears into the combine,

211
00:14:36,700 --> 00:14:38,940
where they're conveyed into the threshing mechanism.

212
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:43,460
Here, a spinning rotor threshes the kernels,

213
00:14:43,960 --> 00:14:45,160
removing them from the cob,

214
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:48,780
while a strong fan blows out the leaves and chaff.

215
00:14:49,580 --> 00:14:52,680
The kernels are then conveyed to the combine's holding tank.

216
00:14:57,820 --> 00:15:00,860
On Sandahl's farm, the corn is offloaded onto trailers.

217
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:06,360
The kernels are loaded into bins that will store the grain

218
00:15:06,361 --> 00:15:07,900
and complete the drying process.

219
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:14,000
Corn is a crop with many uses, everything

220
00:15:14,001 --> 00:15:19,040
from tortillas to corn syrup, animal feed, and ethanol,

221
00:15:19,041 --> 00:15:22,000
a corn-based alternative fuel that is boosting demand

222
00:15:22,001 --> 00:15:23,040
across the Corn Belt.

223
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:31,260
More than 7,000 years ago, it was a decidedly simpler crop.

224
00:15:31,261 --> 00:15:33,320
In the region that is now Mexico,

225
00:15:33,321 --> 00:15:38,760
natives began harvesting a wild grass called teosinte.

226
00:15:38,761 --> 00:15:41,360
Over thousands of years, the Mesoamericans

227
00:15:41,361 --> 00:15:44,200
domesticated teosinte by sowing and reaping

228
00:15:44,201 --> 00:15:47,240
only the best kernels.

229
00:15:47,241 --> 00:15:50,240
it evolved into a hardier, more productive plant,

230
00:15:50,241 --> 00:15:52,420
known as maize, or corn.

231
00:15:54,180 --> 00:15:58,220
After its first migration north at about 1200 BCE,

232
00:15:58,221 --> 00:16:00,020
it would become the most important crop

233
00:16:00,021 --> 00:16:01,780
in American agriculture.

234
00:16:01,781 --> 00:16:03,280
When the pilgrims first arrived,

235
00:16:03,281 --> 00:16:06,400
they planted in small hills, several seeds in a hill.

236
00:16:06,401 --> 00:16:08,500
They grew the corn, they harvested the years,

237
00:16:08,501 --> 00:16:10,000
and they ground it in a flower.

238
00:16:12,340 --> 00:16:15,460
In the colonies, and for centuries that followed,

239
00:16:15,461 --> 00:16:17,060
corn was harvested by hand.

240
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:22,900
The corn knife with an 18 inch blade

241
00:16:22,901 --> 00:16:25,500
was one of the original methods of cutting the stalks.

242
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:29,880
When farmers were picking only the ears,

243
00:16:29,881 --> 00:16:33,360
hand held hooks or pegs made of iron and bone

244
00:16:33,361 --> 00:16:34,700
were the standard tools.

245
00:16:37,160 --> 00:16:41,060
This is a bone peg that was probably used in 1890.

246
00:16:41,061 --> 00:16:43,340
They used anything that was sharp

247
00:16:43,341 --> 00:16:45,560
to open up the shucks at that time.

248
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:49,420
Gloves with embedded hooks later replaced the peg.

249
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:52,880
People in rural communities are very innovative,

250
00:16:52,881 --> 00:16:54,800
and so they were always looking for some way

251
00:16:54,801 --> 00:16:57,740
to do it better, to do it faster.

252
00:16:57,741 --> 00:17:01,100
The hooks and pegs allowed for faster harvesting

253
00:17:01,101 --> 00:17:04,480
because they could rip the shuck away from the ear,

254
00:17:04,481 --> 00:17:09,100
snap it and harvest it much quicker than just by hand.

255
00:17:09,101 --> 00:17:16,280
Even today, many rural communities still celebrate the days of hand harvesting with

256
00:17:16,281 --> 00:17:20,840
husking bee competitions.

257
00:17:20,841 --> 00:17:23,420
Ted Martin is a former Nebraska state champion.

258
00:17:23,421 --> 00:17:24,520
Ted Martin, Nebraska State Chief of the Air

259
00:17:24,521 --> 00:17:30,440
Well, you grab the ear towards the end like this and come in with the hook, crossways,

260
00:17:30,441 --> 00:17:37,380
open up the shucks, then you peel them back and you snap the ear out and throw it.

261
00:17:37,381 --> 00:17:39,980
and it's all one motion, really.

262
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:47,880
A fast husker could generally harvest about 100 bushels,

263
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:49,880
or one acre of corn per day.

264
00:17:51,880 --> 00:17:55,080
But enormous advancements in agricultural technology

265
00:17:55,081 --> 00:17:57,800
allows modern farmers to dwarf this number.

266
00:17:59,640 --> 00:18:01,620
Now we return to the fields,

267
00:18:01,621 --> 00:18:04,460
here on Modern Marvel's Harvesting Plus.

268
00:18:04,461 --> 00:18:10,460
Tillers, combines, cultivators are just some of the innovative

269
00:18:10,461 --> 00:18:14,460
pieces of farming gear that have revolutionized harvesting

270
00:18:14,461 --> 00:18:16,460
in the modern era.

271
00:18:16,461 --> 00:18:19,460
But long before machines like tractors and harvesters took

272
00:18:19,461 --> 00:18:23,460
over, farming meant putting your shoulder to the wheel.

273
00:18:23,461 --> 00:18:27,460
For decades, nearly every farm was equipped with a hand-operated

274
00:18:27,461 --> 00:18:29,460
corn sheller.

275
00:18:29,461 --> 00:18:33,460
Okay, we've got a couple cobs of corn, and we're looking at a

276
00:18:33,461 --> 00:18:37,460
We've got a hand-powered corn sheller that's about 100 years old,

277
00:18:37,461 --> 00:18:39,460
and we're going to run a couple cobs through it.

278
00:18:39,461 --> 00:18:41,460
First, I'm going to crank it up.

279
00:18:41,461 --> 00:18:45,460
A couple plates inside the machine, going in opposite directions,

280
00:18:45,461 --> 00:18:47,460
are going to shear the kernels off.

281
00:18:51,460 --> 00:18:53,460
The corn kernels go down the bottom.

282
00:18:53,461 --> 00:18:56,460
The corn cobs come out the other end.

283
00:18:56,461 --> 00:19:01,460
And there's a fan at the bottom that blows some of the chaff away as well.

284
00:19:01,461 --> 00:19:06,900
Many farmers harvested corn for silage, or animal feed.

285
00:19:06,901 --> 00:19:10,460
This included the stalk as well as the ear.

286
00:19:10,461 --> 00:19:15,900
Silage was the first corn harvest to see mechanization

287
00:19:15,901 --> 00:19:19,620
in the form of the corn binder in the late 1800s.

288
00:19:19,621 --> 00:19:25,620
On one Nebraska farm, Ron Lang operates an antique corn binder

289
00:19:25,621 --> 00:19:28,620
to harvest feed for his horses.

290
00:19:28,621 --> 00:19:35,940
It's an international corn binder. It presses it and ties it.

291
00:19:35,941 --> 00:19:39,180
This mainly used to be used

292
00:19:39,181 --> 00:19:42,700
more for green corn than fill silos with it.

293
00:19:42,701 --> 00:19:45,980
They would cut the corn green and haul it

294
00:19:45,981 --> 00:19:50,380
in and fill your silos.

295
00:19:50,381 --> 00:19:56,289
The binder both cuts and then automatically ties the stalks into bundles with twine.

296
00:19:56,290 --> 00:19:56,990
The

297
00:19:56,991 --> 00:20:05,260
The operator uses a foot clutch to engage a conveyor that drops the bundles in the field.

298
00:20:05,261 --> 00:20:10,220
Corn binders were usually followed by a crew of laborers who stood the bundles into larger,

299
00:20:10,221 --> 00:20:14,300
upright stacks, known as shocks.

300
00:20:14,301 --> 00:20:18,340
According to Ron, this traditional shock building technique prevents mold from growing in the

301
00:20:18,341 --> 00:20:21,820
bundles, which helps keep the feed from rotting.

302
00:20:21,821 --> 00:20:28,820
Shocking is for your winter feed and it would mold and stuff on the ground.

303
00:20:28,821 --> 00:20:33,820
And the shocking is for curing. You gotta shock it so the wind goes through it.

304
00:20:33,821 --> 00:20:42,820
Up through the Great Depression, the meticulous hand harvest was still the only way to pick the ears directly.

305
00:20:42,821 --> 00:20:53,420
The first mechanized corn pickers, powered by internal combustion engines, debuted in the late 1930s and would soon double productivity to 15 acres in one day.

306
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:05,460
The pickers selectively harvest only the corn ears, depositing the stalks back in the field.

307
00:21:05,461 --> 00:21:17,160
Farmer Randy Jensen uses a two-row 1960s model 237 John Deere picker to harvest 600 acres of corn each year.

308
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:23,240
The picker uses rollers to snap the ears off the stalks.

309
00:21:24,980 --> 00:21:29,460
The ears travel to a husking bag where rubber rollers remove the husks.

310
00:21:30,620 --> 00:21:32,380
The clean ears then drop in a wagon.

311
00:21:32,920 --> 00:21:35,440
When you come to the end, you'll have a full wagon most of the time.

312
00:21:35,460 --> 00:21:39,280
and you'll have a guy at the other end and he'll switch wagons with you

313
00:21:39,281 --> 00:21:43,600
and he'll take it home and run it to an elevator

314
00:21:43,601 --> 00:21:45,620
which elevates it up into an ear corn crib

315
00:21:45,621 --> 00:21:48,800
and basically that's all you do.

316
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:51,880
While the mechanical pickers endured for decades,

317
00:21:51,881 --> 00:21:55,600
they couldn't shell clean and dry corn immediately in the field.

318
00:21:56,220 --> 00:21:59,440
By the mid-1950s, there was an answer.

319
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:04,560
The combine, which had long before mechanized the harvesting of other grain crops,

320
00:22:04,561 --> 00:22:09,720
was now advanced enough to produce clean corn kernels at a staggering pace.

321
00:22:12,120 --> 00:22:19,920
What began with two-row designs grew to the 12 and even 16-row corn head combines of the 21st century.

322
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:24,240
You're talking about the ability to harvest 4,000 or so bushels an hour,

323
00:22:24,241 --> 00:22:33,460
which translates to maybe 60 to 70 pounds of grain, clean grain, every second that this machine can harvest.

324
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:37,600
If we go back to working with a corn binder,

325
00:22:37,601 --> 00:22:41,360
we're talking about working maybe eight acres a day,

326
00:22:41,361 --> 00:22:43,840
and now we're talking about hand picking,

327
00:22:43,841 --> 00:22:47,080
we're talking an acre or so a day, 100 bushels.

328
00:22:47,081 --> 00:22:50,040
Today's big farmers with large combines

329
00:22:50,041 --> 00:22:53,580
can do 40,000 bushels in a day with one combine.

330
00:22:55,720 --> 00:22:59,080
The American corn harvest relies on the size, speed,

331
00:22:59,081 --> 00:23:01,260
and sophistication of one machine.

332
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:10,200
Every year in October, there is a fruit that must first go for a swim before it can be picked.

333
00:23:13,800 --> 00:23:19,760
Just days before harvesting, farmers in central Wisconsin divert water from nearby reservoirs

334
00:23:19,761 --> 00:23:24,440
in order to flood thousands of acres of cranberries in up to two feet of water.

335
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:28,520
This is called a wet harvest.

336
00:23:30,960 --> 00:23:33,220
The berries grow on short vines.

337
00:23:33,221 --> 00:23:37,880
When they mature, the fields are flooded to make harvesting easier.

338
00:23:41,960 --> 00:23:45,300
These water reel harvesters, also called beaters,

339
00:23:45,560 --> 00:23:48,840
will transform the marsh into a sea of red.

340
00:23:49,340 --> 00:23:51,640
The first guy that goes in the water is opening the bed,

341
00:23:52,800 --> 00:23:56,180
and then the rest of the guys behind him just keep following along behind him.

342
00:23:56,740 --> 00:24:00,500
What they're looking for in the water is what we call a berry line,

343
00:24:00,501 --> 00:24:03,320
where you can see where the fruit's been picked clean

344
00:24:03,321 --> 00:24:05,040
and where the remaining fruit is.

345
00:24:05,080 --> 00:24:06,400
So you try to line up on that,

346
00:24:06,580 --> 00:24:08,560
and the idea is to not miss any fruit

347
00:24:08,561 --> 00:24:10,600
and beat it clean or pick it clean.

348
00:24:12,080 --> 00:24:13,920
As the beaters move through the vine,

349
00:24:14,140 --> 00:24:15,320
they strip off the berries.

350
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:19,700
Cranberries contain four air chambers.

351
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:21,640
When freed from the vines,

352
00:24:21,940 --> 00:24:24,020
their natural buoyancy causes them to float.

353
00:24:25,040 --> 00:24:26,400
Cranberries are trained,

354
00:24:26,820 --> 00:24:29,200
meaning the vines lay a certain direction.

355
00:24:29,201 --> 00:24:32,540
A good analogy would be like petting a cat the right way and the wrong way.

356
00:24:32,541 --> 00:24:34,200
You pet it the right way, everything's nice.

357
00:24:34,201 --> 00:24:36,200
You go against the vines or against that cat,

358
00:24:36,201 --> 00:24:39,200
and every fur gets all ruffled up and it's not a very nice thing.

359
00:24:39,201 --> 00:24:41,200
Same thing can happen with cranberries.

360
00:24:41,201 --> 00:24:45,200
If we go against the vines, we'd rip them out of the ground and it would be a mess.

361
00:24:49,200 --> 00:24:53,200
Once the three-acre bed is beaten, the workers deploy a cran boom

362
00:24:53,201 --> 00:24:56,200
to slowly coax the flotilla to one end of the marsh.

363
00:24:59,200 --> 00:25:06,200
Here, more than 80,000 pounds of berries are loaded by elevator and trucked out.

364
00:25:06,201 --> 00:25:20,200
At the Ocean Spray Receiving Station, the berries are offloaded into holding tanks

365
00:25:20,201 --> 00:25:28,200
and then conveyed into the packing house, where they're cleaned and then sized.

366
00:25:28,201 --> 00:25:36,920
These cranberries are destined for processing, meaning they will end up as juice, sauce, or even craisins, the cranberry equivalent of raisins.

367
00:25:38,860 --> 00:25:51,247
But long before cranberry juice and craisins dominated the market, Native Americans understood the power of the fresh berries, using them in medicines, poultices, and

368
00:25:51,248 --> 00:25:51,948
dyes.

369
00:25:51,949 --> 00:25:55,760
Dried berries were even pressed together with venison and fat

370
00:25:55,761 --> 00:25:58,800
to produce pemmican, a survival cake

371
00:25:58,801 --> 00:26:00,960
that kept for long periods of time.

372
00:26:04,160 --> 00:26:07,120
The first commercial growers in the 19th century

373
00:26:07,121 --> 00:26:10,780
recruited cheap field laborers, often women and children,

374
00:26:11,260 --> 00:26:14,260
to meticulously hand-harvest the wild-growing vines.

375
00:26:14,920 --> 00:26:19,000
When the men, women, and children would show up on the cranberry marsh,

376
00:26:19,001 --> 00:26:21,320
they were given a measure.

377
00:26:21,860 --> 00:26:23,380
Once they had a full measure,

378
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:25,500
they would go over to the edge of the bed,

379
00:26:25,940 --> 00:26:28,800
dump the measure into the receptacle,

380
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:31,080
and that picker was given a picking chip.

381
00:26:31,480 --> 00:26:34,040
That picking chip would be used

382
00:26:34,041 --> 00:26:37,820
for either cash or merchandise at the company store.

383
00:26:39,420 --> 00:26:42,900
In the 1890s, the first cranberry handrake

384
00:26:42,901 --> 00:26:44,880
replaced the picker's calloused fingers

385
00:26:44,881 --> 00:26:47,800
and quickly became the harvesting method of choice.

386
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:54,000
Brewers eventually discovered that flooding the cranberry beds made hand raking much easier.

387
00:26:54,001 --> 00:26:58,000
This is the hand rake. It's got metal teeth with slots in it.

388
00:26:58,001 --> 00:27:03,000
As you rake, you go through the berries, through the vines like that.

389
00:27:03,001 --> 00:27:06,000
The berries are floating on top of the water.

390
00:27:06,001 --> 00:27:10,000
They're attached to the vines, but you still got to take them off.

391
00:27:10,001 --> 00:27:16,000
And it's a back-breaking job if you have to do it all day long, especially if the vines aren't trained.

392
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:26,500
In the 1950s, Wisconsinite Leonard Getzinger designed one of the first successful mechanical

393
00:27:26,501 --> 00:27:27,372
harvesters.

394
00:27:27,373 --> 00:27:33,480
The hand-pushed Getzinger picker uses retractable metal teeth to comb through

395
00:27:33,481 --> 00:27:36,530
the vines and pick berries.

396
00:27:36,531 --> 00:27:43,420
Up until the 1970s, the majority of all cranberries were sold

397
00:27:43,421 --> 00:27:45,560
as fresh fruit for traditional cooking.

398
00:27:47,360 --> 00:27:52,820
During the 1970s and 1980s, the juice market exploded,

399
00:27:53,440 --> 00:27:57,760
and the demand for cranberry juice far exceeded the demand

400
00:27:58,100 --> 00:27:59,900
for fresh cranberries.

401
00:28:00,280 --> 00:28:04,700
The whole industry shifted from fresh fruit to processed fruit.

402
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:08,920
Starting in the 70s and 80s, most farmers began wet harvesting

403
00:28:08,921 --> 00:28:10,920
with beaders to bring in a larger,

404
00:28:11,240 --> 00:28:12,940
yet less carefully handled crop.

405
00:28:13,420 --> 00:28:20,920
But today, one man feels there is still a better mousetrap for farmers who raise cranberries

406
00:28:20,921 --> 00:28:22,600
for processing.

407
00:28:22,601 --> 00:28:24,600
It's called the Ruby Slipper.

408
00:28:24,601 --> 00:28:32,700
It's a machine I invented trying to find a simpler, faster method to harvest cranberries

409
00:28:32,701 --> 00:28:37,260
that was easier on the fruit, easier on the vines.

410
00:28:37,261 --> 00:28:38,260
This is a lot gentler.

411
00:28:38,261 --> 00:28:43,040
The bar itself is traveling at a slower speed when it contacts the vine, but the ground

412
00:28:43,041 --> 00:28:44,840
The speed of the machine is a lot faster.

413
00:28:48,280 --> 00:28:50,660
The ruby slipper is pulled through the marsh

414
00:28:50,661 --> 00:28:53,380
and requires no moving parts to remove the cranberries.

415
00:28:56,660 --> 00:28:59,340
Regardless of the machine, harvesting cranberries

416
00:28:59,341 --> 00:29:02,320
for juice or craisins is not a gentle affair.

417
00:29:07,320 --> 00:29:10,360
But there's another harvest, more steeped in tradition,

418
00:29:10,361 --> 00:29:13,120
that demands a more delicate touch.

419
00:29:13,121 --> 00:29:15,600
Habelman Brothers in Tomah, Wisconsin,

420
00:29:15,601 --> 00:29:19,160
is one of the world's largest producers of fresh cranberries.

421
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:24,900
This harvester is all about delivering the perfect berry.

422
00:29:27,140 --> 00:29:30,320
Habelman's harvesters utilize stainless steel teeth

423
00:29:30,321 --> 00:29:34,020
to comb the vines, much like the original Getzinger pickers.

424
00:29:35,520 --> 00:29:39,540
But these gentler modern harvesters use a series of paddles

425
00:29:39,541 --> 00:29:41,240
to free the berries from the vines.

426
00:29:42,180 --> 00:29:45,080
The berries never end up back in the water.

427
00:29:45,081 --> 00:29:48,020
They go right into the boats and then into the trucks.

428
00:29:48,021 --> 00:29:50,260
We can do about 10 acres a day

429
00:29:50,261 --> 00:29:52,620
with the two machines that we're using right now.

430
00:29:55,260 --> 00:29:56,780
When it's time for packing,

431
00:29:56,781 --> 00:29:59,000
the berries go through a separator line

432
00:29:59,001 --> 00:30:02,340
to remove discolored and poor quality berries.

433
00:30:02,341 --> 00:30:04,960
At the front end is an antique device

434
00:30:04,961 --> 00:30:08,260
that has never been upgraded or redesigned,

435
00:30:08,261 --> 00:30:10,140
the Bailey separator.

436
00:30:10,141 --> 00:30:13,940
Invented by the H.R. Bailey Company in 1917,

437
00:30:13,941 --> 00:30:15,900
it was conceived with the idea

438
00:30:15,901 --> 00:30:19,020
that only fresh cranberries will bounce.

439
00:30:19,021 --> 00:30:21,180
So they pass through a series of boards.

440
00:30:21,181 --> 00:30:23,860
Good ones jump and land on a finish belt.

441
00:30:23,861 --> 00:30:26,080
The bad ones just kind of filter down the machine.

442
00:30:26,081 --> 00:30:27,920
And a lot of companies have tried to come up

443
00:30:27,921 --> 00:30:31,080
with a better design, but there isn't any out there yet.

444
00:30:32,820 --> 00:30:35,000
The cranberries that pass Bailey's tests

445
00:30:35,001 --> 00:30:37,960
are then double-checked by more modern equipment.

446
00:30:37,961 --> 00:30:40,820
two different electronic separators use ultraviolet

447
00:30:40,821 --> 00:30:44,680
and fluorescent light to detect inferior color and rot.

448
00:30:48,240 --> 00:30:51,280
After the antique and high-tech separators have their go,

449
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:53,480
the final quality check is human.

450
00:30:54,600 --> 00:30:56,660
Any bad berries that have survived the gauntlet

451
00:30:56,661 --> 00:30:58,420
are weeded out before packaging.

452
00:31:00,520 --> 00:31:03,880
As the demand for healthier food and drink continues to spiral,

453
00:31:04,520 --> 00:31:07,180
a wild assortment of cranberry harvesting machines

454
00:31:07,181 --> 00:31:10,600
will continue to prowl the marshes of central Wisconsin.

455
00:31:15,860 --> 00:31:19,300
In the low desert of Southern California's Coachella Valley,

456
00:31:19,680 --> 00:31:23,980
there's an annual ritual that is perhaps the most dangerous harvest in America.

457
00:31:29,760 --> 00:31:31,760
This man is a palmero.

458
00:31:33,180 --> 00:31:36,660
His job is to harvest dates from trees that can grow to heights

459
00:31:36,661 --> 00:31:39,760
of more than 80 feet.

460
00:31:39,761 --> 00:31:45,700
Date grower Dennis Jensen speaks of the industry's anxiety over the ladder harvest.

461
00:31:45,701 --> 00:31:46,880
It's dangerous work up there.

462
00:31:46,881 --> 00:31:49,860
One slip and they could end up in a world of hurt.

463
00:31:49,861 --> 00:31:57,140
As much as we try safety-wise, it seems that every other year we lose someone to this terrible

464
00:31:57,141 --> 00:31:59,400
tragedy.

465
00:31:59,401 --> 00:32:03,640
Some of the other items that are very dangerous are the spines, the palm fronds themselves

466
00:32:03,641 --> 00:32:07,380
that can pierce your hand or an eye or an arm or a leg or foot.

467
00:32:07,381 --> 00:32:09,620
And they're located throughout the crown of that tree.

468
00:32:09,621 --> 00:32:12,420
So there's a lot of different dangers that they have to work with.

469
00:32:14,860 --> 00:32:19,420
The palmeros in this field are hand harvesting a premium date, known as the medjool.

470
00:32:20,560 --> 00:32:24,660
One of several date varieties that have been cultivated since before recorded time.

471
00:32:29,100 --> 00:32:31,800
Dates are likely the oldest of all harvested fruits.

472
00:32:33,640 --> 00:32:40,640
Nearly 6,000 years ago, they were cultivated in the region of Mesopotamia, or what is now modern Iraq.

473
00:32:40,641 --> 00:32:44,640
Some traditions trace the fruit to the Garden of Eden.

474
00:32:44,641 --> 00:32:54,640
Even the Bible refers to the land of milk and honey, and we've been told by Jewish scholars that the honey it was referring to was the honey of the date palms.

475
00:32:54,641 --> 00:33:01,640
Its ability to grow and produce fruit in such an arid environment made the date palm a sacred tree.

476
00:33:01,641 --> 00:33:06,400
Dates keep very well and in that hot desert area where they had no

477
00:33:06,401 --> 00:33:10,600
refrigeration they could keep them for a long time and early on they discovered

478
00:33:10,601 --> 00:33:14,560
the ways to press them together to get rid of air and so forth so that they

479
00:33:14,561 --> 00:33:20,680
would keep. After many failed attempts date palm offshoots were successfully

480
00:33:20,681 --> 00:33:27,480
imported and planted in the Arizona desert in the year 1900. The coveted

481
00:33:27,481 --> 00:33:32,940
The medjool offshoots came from Morocco in 1927 and were later planted in the Coachella

482
00:33:32,941 --> 00:33:34,440
Valley.

483
00:33:34,441 --> 00:33:41,460
Today, California growers of medjools and the other major variety, the deglet noor, oversee

484
00:33:41,461 --> 00:33:49,560
a thriving industry that annually harvests a crop worth around $50 million.

485
00:33:49,561 --> 00:33:51,900
And it's not just the fruit that is valuable.

486
00:33:51,901 --> 00:33:57,040
The date palm is considered so beautiful that as many as 15,000 trees are harvested and

487
00:33:57,041 --> 00:34:01,080
sold each year to real estate developers.

488
00:34:01,081 --> 00:34:04,820
But times are changing for the skilled palmeros.

489
00:34:04,821 --> 00:34:07,840
Most date growers are relying less on the latter harvest

490
00:34:07,841 --> 00:34:11,660
and more on a mechanical harvesting aid, the canasta.

491
00:34:15,460 --> 00:34:18,060
Mounted on a nine-ton lift, the platform

492
00:34:18,061 --> 00:34:20,460
opens and closes around the date palm's trunk.

493
00:34:23,660 --> 00:34:26,780
Even as mechanization makes the work safer and easier,

494
00:34:26,781 --> 00:34:29,360
the job still requires a trained palmero

495
00:34:29,361 --> 00:34:31,520
to distinguish dates that are ripe for picking.

496
00:34:33,160 --> 00:34:34,300
Normally what they're gonna do

497
00:34:34,301 --> 00:34:36,700
is they'll be looking for the ripest ones

498
00:34:36,701 --> 00:34:38,460
and they're pretty easy to pull off.

499
00:34:38,461 --> 00:34:39,800
And sometimes you can just shake them

500
00:34:39,801 --> 00:34:41,780
and they'll actually fall off of the bunches.

501
00:34:41,781 --> 00:34:43,160
But they'll be pulling out the ripe ones,

502
00:34:43,161 --> 00:34:44,360
which are the darker color ones.

503
00:34:44,361 --> 00:34:47,120
There's yellow ones on here, which are not ripe yet.

504
00:34:47,121 --> 00:34:49,260
It'll take another probably two to three weeks

505
00:34:49,261 --> 00:34:50,660
before they will ripen up.

506
00:34:52,740 --> 00:34:54,740
Prior to harvesting, the palmeros wrap

507
00:34:54,741 --> 00:34:57,640
each and every growing date bunch in a nylon bag.

508
00:34:58,640 --> 00:35:03,580
Our date bags are used to keep moisture away from the dates

509
00:35:03,581 --> 00:35:06,120
should we have a sudden thunderstorm.

510
00:35:06,121 --> 00:35:09,520
And the rain and moisture causes a lot of decay for the dates.

511
00:35:09,521 --> 00:35:13,760
Additional purposes of these bags are to keep insects away

512
00:35:13,761 --> 00:35:15,760
and to keep birds from eating the dates.

513
00:35:19,700 --> 00:35:21,840
To prevent damage to the delicate fruit,

514
00:35:21,841 --> 00:35:24,680
harvesters lower small baskets to the ground,

515
00:35:24,681 --> 00:35:26,440
where they're hand-sorted for ripeness.

516
00:35:30,180 --> 00:35:32,060
After a short journey to Dennis Jensen's

517
00:35:32,061 --> 00:35:34,660
Seaview Packing House, the dates are cooled.

518
00:35:36,100 --> 00:35:37,740
Then they're carefully washed and dried

519
00:35:37,741 --> 00:35:39,620
before they're hand-sorted for packing.

520
00:35:43,620 --> 00:35:46,360
One of five quality categories are assigned

521
00:35:46,361 --> 00:35:48,960
before the dates are shipped to points around the world.

522
00:35:54,680 --> 00:35:57,000
Welcome back to Harvesting Plus.

523
00:35:58,900 --> 00:36:01,260
Today, Kennett Square, Pennsylvania,

524
00:36:01,640 --> 00:36:03,820
is known as the mushroom capital of the world.

525
00:36:04,540 --> 00:36:07,020
And not long ago, the world's largest mushroom grower

526
00:36:07,021 --> 00:36:09,720
was Creekside Mushrooms, located near Pittsburgh.

527
00:36:10,940 --> 00:36:13,680
Creekside owners have since moved on to other ventures,

528
00:36:13,940 --> 00:36:17,440
but for many years, its 800 acres of limestone tunnels

529
00:36:17,441 --> 00:36:19,880
burrowed beneath trees and pasture land

530
00:36:19,881 --> 00:36:21,960
was a fungus farmer's fantasy.

531
00:36:21,961 --> 00:36:28,200
The neat thing about growing mushrooms at this particular facility is that the temperature

532
00:36:28,201 --> 00:36:33,760
is pretty much consistent all year round, and we maintain about a 62 degree temperature

533
00:36:33,761 --> 00:36:40,960
all year round, and about a 95% humidity, which allows mushrooms to just recycle themselves

534
00:36:40,961 --> 00:36:42,580
pretty much every 24 hours.

535
00:36:44,860 --> 00:36:50,740
Here, the fungus of choice is the sylvan white agaricus, the ever-popular white button mushroom.

536
00:36:51,960 --> 00:37:02,000
A small army of harvesters speeds from one cave to the next, each worker aiming to pick

537
00:37:02,001 --> 00:37:08,340
up a minimum of 55 pounds an hour.

538
00:37:08,341 --> 00:37:12,640
Mushrooms are grown in and harvested from large wooden trays.

539
00:37:12,641 --> 00:37:16,840
Each tray is harvested in passes or breaks.

540
00:37:16,841 --> 00:37:21,020
The first break yields the large and medium mushrooms.

541
00:37:21,021 --> 00:37:26,520
Removing the larger fungi allows more room for the smaller mushrooms to grow and develop.

542
00:37:26,521 --> 00:37:34,700
I'm picking the mushrooms that are ready at the prime time for harvest, and that depends

543
00:37:34,701 --> 00:37:41,120
upon the stem length, the shape of the cap, whether it's domed or oval.

544
00:37:41,121 --> 00:37:46,640
If you notice, the only smalls that I take are the ones that come off basically by accident.

545
00:37:47,720 --> 00:37:49,460
They're left on there for tomorrow.

546
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:52,120
There'll be mushrooms that will be harvested again tomorrow.

547
00:37:53,300 --> 00:37:58,380
Each tray ultimately yields approximately 250 pounds of organic mushrooms.

548
00:37:59,060 --> 00:38:00,560
But not every mushroom is perfect.

549
00:38:01,180 --> 00:38:03,740
This mushroom in particular has a blemish on it.

550
00:38:04,700 --> 00:38:06,940
That is what we consider a number two.

551
00:38:07,140 --> 00:38:10,660
It would not look as nice when it hits the supermarket.

552
00:38:11,120 --> 00:38:19,060
The popular white agaricus mushroom was first domesticated in the cellars and sewers of

553
00:38:19,061 --> 00:38:21,340
19th century France.

554
00:38:21,341 --> 00:38:29,480
The process of producing the spawn, or seed, was the key to growing the fungus commercially.

555
00:38:29,481 --> 00:38:34,500
After the French perfected a strain of spawn, it was soon exported to England, and ultimately

556
00:38:34,501 --> 00:38:37,720
to the United States.

557
00:38:37,721 --> 00:38:42,860
The mushroom business took off in 1904 when the United States Department of Agriculture

558
00:38:42,861 --> 00:38:50,440
perfected a domestically bred spawn, one of the very same strains that are used today.

559
00:38:50,441 --> 00:38:56,160
Even with a healthy spawn, profitability growing mushrooms in vast numbers is a very demanding

560
00:38:56,161 --> 00:38:57,440
craft.

561
00:38:57,441 --> 00:39:02,420
The process begins with piles of hay, straw, and manure.

562
00:39:02,421 --> 00:39:11,120
This compost is turned, adding water and fresh air to speed up decomposition.

563
00:39:11,121 --> 00:39:14,940
The compost is then pasteurized in a fresh air tunnel system.

564
00:39:14,941 --> 00:39:20,100
It kills all the other microorganisms that are in the compost so that when we do plant

565
00:39:20,101 --> 00:39:27,540
the spawn or the mushroom seeds into the tray, it gives a nice clean medium for the spawn

566
00:39:27,541 --> 00:39:32,260
to take hold inside the compost.

567
00:39:32,261 --> 00:39:36,260
Each tray is filled with compost, and the spawn is then added.

568
00:39:36,261 --> 00:39:39,360
The trays are transported deep within the pitch dark limestone

569
00:39:39,361 --> 00:39:43,640
caves to begin coaxing the root structure, or mycelia,

570
00:39:43,641 --> 00:39:44,420
to the surface.

571
00:39:49,860 --> 00:39:51,960
This tray of organic white agaricus

572
00:39:51,961 --> 00:39:54,160
will begin harvesting around day 15.

573
00:39:58,160 --> 00:40:01,340
Its many mushrooms then travel to a packing facility,

574
00:40:01,341 --> 00:40:05,120
where they're weighed, packaged, or sometimes sliced

575
00:40:05,121 --> 00:40:07,880
before they're cooled and quickly shipped to market.

576
00:40:09,740 --> 00:40:11,960
We don't get a whole lot of lead time,

577
00:40:11,961 --> 00:40:13,720
nor do we want a whole lot of lead time

578
00:40:13,721 --> 00:40:15,480
because what we produce today,

579
00:40:15,481 --> 00:40:18,360
we want to make sure we ship within the next 24 hours.

580
00:40:20,420 --> 00:40:22,920
While the mushroom harvest may seem stable,

581
00:40:22,921 --> 00:40:24,660
its future is not.

582
00:40:24,661 --> 00:40:26,460
People that get out of high school today

583
00:40:26,461 --> 00:40:28,000
look for other opportunities,

584
00:40:28,001 --> 00:40:30,880
So it gets harder and harder to find labor.

585
00:40:32,080 --> 00:40:35,740
Today, mushroom growers have only 75% of the workforce

586
00:40:35,741 --> 00:40:37,960
needed to harvest at full capacity.

587
00:40:39,500 --> 00:40:42,480
Mushrooms are not the only crop impacted by a lack of labor.

588
00:40:43,280 --> 00:40:46,140
The trend is affecting harvests across the nation.

589
00:40:47,960 --> 00:40:50,160
There's certainly less labor available.

590
00:40:50,800 --> 00:40:54,080
It began several years ago, but the increased pressure

591
00:40:54,081 --> 00:40:58,520
to reduce illegal immigration across the southern United States border

592
00:40:58,521 --> 00:41:01,880
has influenced the amount of laborers that are available for harvest.

593
00:41:04,640 --> 00:41:06,500
As workers become scarcer,

594
00:41:07,920 --> 00:41:12,820
mechanization will play a larger, but probably still restricted role in the harvest.

595
00:41:13,720 --> 00:41:16,280
Labor is the most important factor in this business.

596
00:41:16,940 --> 00:41:20,200
When you look at the fragility or the nature of the fruit,

597
00:41:20,201 --> 00:41:24,340
this is not something that is conducive to mechanical harvesting.

598
00:41:25,640 --> 00:41:30,480
We rely very heavily on a labor force that's skilled to discern fruit quality on the tree,

599
00:41:30,680 --> 00:41:33,680
and there's really no other way to go about doing that.

600
00:41:35,920 --> 00:41:39,720
Truly, our machines will become more sophisticated and sensitive,

601
00:41:40,380 --> 00:41:44,040
and a declining labor force will spur that mechanical innovation.

602
00:41:44,041 --> 00:41:50,480
But only time will tell if the machine will ever match the human hand's ability

603
00:41:50,481 --> 00:41:54,060
to delicately pluck an unblemished piece of fruit.

